---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "kirkland protein powder",
      "kirkland signature protein powder",
      "kirkland whey protein",
      "costco kirkland protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Best Costco Protein Powder Picks",
    link: "/best-costco-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Kirkland Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Kirkland Signature whey is a plain, sucralose-sweetened blend with about 25 g of protein in 130 calories, at roughly $0.70 per 25 g. The catches: members only, not on Amazon, and no third-party sport certification.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#lineup">The Kirkland Lineup</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Amazon Alternatives</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in the Bag</a>
      <a href="#who-makes-it">Who Makes It</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing Status</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs-rivals">Kirkland vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Kirkland Signature Whey Protein is a normal, sucralose-sweetened whey blend with about 25 g of protein in a 130-calorie scoop, and at the warehouse prices reported over the past two years it costs roughly $0.70 per 25 g of protein. That is about half what the big name-brand tubs cost on Amazon at the time of writing. The catches are real, though: it is a members-only product, Costco does not sell it on Amazon, the manufacturer is not named, and we could not find any third-party sport certification. This review covers the whole Kirkland protein lineup, reads the label line by line, does the membership maths, and lists the closest Amazon alternatives if you cannot or will not shop at Costco.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict on Kirkland Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a Costco member who drinks a shake most days, Kirkland Signature Whey is hard to beat on price. The label is ordinary in a good way: about 25 g of protein, 130 calories, 4 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of sugar in a 35 g scoop, from a blend led by whey protein isolate. That puts it within a gram or two of Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard, the reference whey most people compare against, at roughly half the cost per gram of Gold Standard's Amazon price when we checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            What you give up is transparency and choice. Costco does not name the company that makes the powder. We could not find it in the NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice databases. US warehouses carry one flavor, Creamy Chocolate, in one size, a 5.4 lb bag of about 70 servings. And it is sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium, which some buyers specifically want to avoid. None of these are reasons to distrust the powder, but each one is a reason a specific buyer might choose something else.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Amazon question comes up constantly, so here is the plain answer: Kirkland Signature protein powder is not sold by Costco on Amazon. When we searched Amazon's catalog for Kirkland Signature whey, every result was another brand, mostly Optimum Nutrition, Nutricost, Now Sports and Dymatize. Kirkland bags that turn up on marketplace sites come from third-party resellers, usually at a markup that wipes out the saving and with no way to check how the bag was stored. That is why the product cards on this page are the closest Amazon alternatives to Kirkland, each clearly labeled as an alternative.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is a label-and-price review. We have not tasted Kirkland whey or the alternatives, so any comments on taste and mixing are summarized buyer feedback, and our full approach is on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> page. This page goes deep on Kirkland alone. For the whole warehouse ranked side by side, including Ascent, Isopure and Orgain, see our <a href="/best-costco-protein-powder/">best Costco protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Every Kirkland Signature Protein Product, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kirkland's protein range is much smaller than the search volume suggests. In the US there is one protein powder: Kirkland Signature Whey Protein, Creamy Chocolate, in a 5.4 lb bag of about 70 servings. It reached warehouses in mid-2024 at $47.99 and then Costco.com at a higher price, according to launch coverage from the supplement news site Stack3d. Two years on, that is still the only Kirkland powder we could find on the US side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Costco Canada lists a second flavor, Kirkland Signature Whey Protein Powder in Vanilla, as a 2.45 kg (5.4 lb) bag of about 70 servings. Per that listing, a 35 g scoop gives 25 g of protein, 130 calories and 1 g of sugar, from the same isolate, concentrate and hydrolysate base, sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium. Some US roundups mention vanilla too, but we could not find a US vanilla listing at the time of writing. If you want vanilla, check the Costco app for your warehouse rather than relying on a list.
          </p>

          <p>
            We could not find a Kirkland Signature plant-based or organic protein powder in either country. Costco fills its plant shelf with other brands: Orgain in most US warehouses, and Leanfit and Vega in Canada. So if you searched for Kirkland plant protein, the honest answer is that it does not exist right now. Orgain is the one Costco stocks most reliably, and our Costco guide explains why its 21 g two-scoop serving makes it pricier per gram than the tub price suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two non-powder products carry the Kirkland name. Kirkland Signature Protein Bars give about 21 g of protein and roughly 190 calories per 60 g bar, per labels reported in nutrition databases, varying slightly by flavor. And in late 2025 Costco Canada launched a Kirkland Signature Protein Shake: 30 g of milk protein, 160 calories and 1 g of sugar per 325 ml carton, sold in an 18-pack for about CA$29.99, per Stack3d. We found no US launch of that shake. If ready-to-drink is what you actually want, our <a href="/best-protein-drink/">best protein drink</a> guide covers the US options.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where sold</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Format</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Protein, Creamy Chocolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">US warehouses, Costco.com</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.4 lb bag, about 70 servings</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Protein Powder, Vanilla</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Costco Canada</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.45 kg bag, about 70 servings</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein Shake (chocolate)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Costco Canada warehouses</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">160</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18 x 325 ml cartons</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein Bars</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">US and Canada</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 190</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Multi-bar boxes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Plant protein powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not found</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">n/a</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">n/a</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">n/a</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Figures come from Costco listings, the bag as transcribed by Open Food Facts, and launch coverage at the time of writing, and they vary by flavor and region. In practice, when people say Kirkland protein powder they mean one product, the chocolate whey bag, and that is what the rest of this review is about.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Closest Amazon Alternatives to Kirkland Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kirkland Signature Whey is not sold by Costco on Amazon, so it cannot have a buy button here. These four cards are the Amazon products that come closest to it, and each is badged as an alternative. We matched on formula (an isolate and concentrate whey blend), protein per scoop, tub size and sweetening, then worked out cost per 25 g of protein from the Amazon price we saw at the time of writing. Every one costs more per gram than Kirkland at warehouse prices, so each card explains what you get for that premium. We have not tasted these products; label figures are per the brands and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is Actually in the Kirkland Bag
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Creamy Chocolate ingredient list, per the bag as transcribed by Open Food Facts at the time of writing, reads: whey protein isolate, whey protein concentrate, alkalized cocoa, acacia, whey protein hydrolysate, oat bran fiber, organic high oleic sunflower oil, organic sunflower lecithin, sea salt, natural flavors, organic rice dextrin, organic rosemary extract, organic guar gum, sucralose, acesulfame potassium and xanthan gum. It contains milk. The Canadian vanilla lists the same base with oat beta-glucan concentrate in place of oat bran fiber.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein comes from three forms of whey. Isolate is listed first, so it is the largest share. Isolate is filtered further than concentrate, which removes most of the lactose and fat. Concentrate keeps more of both, and hydrolysate is whey that has been partly broken down, usually added in small amounts. An isolate-led blend like this suits most mildly lactose-sensitive people, but it is not lactose-free. If regular whey bloats you, read our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide before you buy a 5.4 lb bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the list is there for texture and shelf life. Acacia, guar and xanthan gum thicken the shake. The sunflower oil and rice dextrin give it a creamier body, and the rosemary extract is an antioxidant that stops that oil going stale. The oat fiber explains the 1 g of fiber per scoop. The bag says gluten free, but anyone with celiac disease should check the current bag in hand, since oat ingredients and supplier changes are exactly where gluten questions come from.
          </p>

          <p>
            Per 35 g scoop, the panel lists 130 calories, 2 g of fat (1 g saturated), 4 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of fiber, 1 g of sugar, about 170 mg of sodium and 25 g of protein, and the front of the bag claims 5.6 g of BCAAs. That makes the powder about 71 percent protein by weight, or roughly 19 g of protein per 100 calories. Gold Standard is slightly more concentrated at about 77 percent, because Kirkland spends a few grams per scoop on the gums, oil and fiber. It is a small difference, and it is part of why buyers describe Kirkland as creamy. One nutrition site lists a 43 g, 160-calorie serving that does not match the current bag, so trust the panel on your bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweeteners are sucralose and acesulfame potassium, the same pair used in Aldi's Elevation whey and Dymatize Elite. Both are approved for use in food and are the norm in budget whey. If you would rather avoid them, Kirkland is the wrong product, and you are better off with an unflavored whey you sweeten yourself or a stevia-sweetened brand. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> guide lists the options.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">LABEL CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">What the bag claims, and what it does not</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Kirkland claims gluten free and no added sugar, and the Canadian listing adds no soy ingredients, non-GMO ingredients and no artificial preservatives. It does not name a manufacturer, show a third-party sport certification, or publish heavy-metals results. Costco backs it with the Kirkland Signature satisfaction guarantee instead.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Who Makes Kirkland Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Costco does not name the manufacturer on the bag or on its product pages, and we could not find a reliable public report that does. Several websites confidently name a maker, but none we found cited a source, a facility registration or a statement from Costco or the supplier. Treat any confident answer as speculation until Costco or a credible outlet confirms it.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is normal for a store brand. Costco contracts with suppliers who make Kirkland products to its specifications, and while a few Kirkland items have been publicly linked to well-known makers over the years, protein powder is not one of them. The Costco Canada listing notes that label information comes from suppliers, which tells you the arrangement but not the name. Aldi does the same with Elevation, as our <a href="/aldi-vs-costco-protein-powder/">Aldi vs Costco protein powder</a> comparison explains.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, an unnamed manufacturer means you are trusting Costco's supplier standards and its return policy rather than a brand's public testing record. Costco's satisfaction guarantee is genuinely useful if you hate the flavor or the bag clumps, because you can take it back. It is not the same thing as independent contaminant or banned-substance testing, which is covered in the testing section below.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other practical point is that store brands can change suppliers and formulas with little announcement. Early coverage described the blend in slightly different terms from the current bag, and nutrition databases carry conflicting entries. Read the ingredient list and panel on the bag you actually buy, and check the best-by date before you open it. A 70-serving bag lasts most people two months or more, and our guide on <a href="/does-protein-powder-expire/">whether protein powder expires</a> explains how to store it so the last scoop is as good as the first.
          </p>
        </div>

